# Marquise Cut Bezel Engagement Ring ({{year}}): 3 Protection Rules Over $4K

![Three loose marquise cut diamonds held in jeweller tweezers against a soft grey backdrop as the DiamondCritics featured image for the {{year}} marquise cut bezel engagement ring guide titled 3 protection rules over four thousand dollars](/images/marquise-bezel-engagement-ring.jpg "Marquise Cut Bezel Engagement Ring 3 Protection Rules Over Four Thousand Dollars")

## TL;DR — Marquise Bezel Engagement Ring in One Scroll

**A marquise bezel engagement ring wraps a metal rim around the entire centre stone — no prongs, no exposed tips.** For marquise diamonds, the bezel is the single setting style that eliminates the tip-chip risk entirely. The two sharp pointed tips sit fully inside the metal wall. A doorframe hit does not reach the stone. For buyers who want a marquise engagement ring that survives 50 years of daily wear without maintenance, bezel is the honest answer.

**The 3 protection rules over $4K (all 3 covered in full below):**

- **Rule 1 — Full bezel over partial bezel.** Full bezel wraps 100% of the stone girdle including both tips. Partial bezel wraps only the shoulders — the two tips still exposed. Full bezel = total tip protection. Partial bezel = half-measure.
- **Rule 2 — Platinum over gold for the bezel wall.** The bezel wall absorbs every impact that would have hit the stone. 14k gold walls dent and deform under years of impacts. Platinum stays rigid. For a lifetime ring, platinum bezel is non-negotiable.
- **Rule 3 — Correct bezel height — 1.0 to 1.5 mm above the girdle.** Below 1.0 mm the wall is too thin to absorb impacts. Above 1.5 mm the wall catches on sweaters and door frames. The sweet spot sits in that 0.5 mm window.

